# Customs workshop notebooks and data

Material for the customs breakouts of the workshop **Data Driven Domestic Revenue Mobilization**
(Bangkok, 19–22 October 2026), organised by the IMF Fiscal Affairs Department.

Everything here is synthetic. Every importer, declaration and name is generated, and nothing
describes a real company or person. Answers and facilitator notes are not in this repository.

## Notebooks

Open a notebook in Google Colab, then *Runtime › Run all*. Nothing to install: the first cell loads the
data and the methods of release `data-v8`.

| Session | Notebook |
| --- | --- |
| Breakout 1B · Targeting: from signals to a score | [Open in Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/github/FrancoisChastel/CustomsWorkshopNotebooks/blob/data-v8/notebooks/1b_targeting.ipynb) |
| Plenary 8 · Six questions, six numbers | [Open in Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/github/FrancoisChastel/CustomsWorkshopNotebooks/blob/data-v8/notebooks/p8_quick_diagnostics.ipynb) |
| Breakout 2B · Trade and the border | [Open in Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/github/FrancoisChastel/CustomsWorkshopNotebooks/blob/data-v8/notebooks/2b_trade_border.ipynb) |
| Breakout 4B · Customs and tax together | [Open in Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/github/FrancoisChastel/CustomsWorkshopNotebooks/blob/data-v8/notebooks/4b_informality.ipynb) |

The exercises end with a reveal that only the facilitator can open.

## What is here

| Folder | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| `notebooks/` | The four Colab notebooks |
| `data/` | One generated world (DATASET-v3): 250,000 declaration lines over 36 months in two halves, the precomputed signals and scores, the importer register, the tax side, the trade tables, the valuation track, the real-register matching files, the session worksheets (JSON), the Excel workbook with every line and every table, and the data dictionary ([data/README.md](data/README.md)) with its calibration lines |
| `workshop_methods/` | The methods used in the sessions, as a small Python package |

Two halves of time: `declarations_h1.csv` (months 1–18) carries the legacy lanes and their control
outcomes; `declarations_h2.csv` (months 19–36) carries no lanes and only the random draw with its
outcomes. An empty `outcome` means no control took place, never "no finding". Nothing heavy runs in
Colab: the signals, the learned score, the oddness score, the clusters, the valuation neighbours and
the match scores are precomputed in `features_lines.csv`, `trader_features.csv`,
`valuation_neighbours.csv` and `match_scores.csv`.

## Use it in Python or Google Colab

```python
!git clone -q https://github.com/FrancoisChastel/CustomsWorkshopNotebooks.git
%cd CustomsWorkshopNotebooks
!pip install -q .

from workshop_methods.data import load_tables, load_declarations
tables = load_tables("data")
lines = load_declarations("data")   # both halves in one frame
```

Without cloning, `load_tables("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FrancoisChastel/CustomsWorkshopNotebooks/main/data")`
reads the CSV files over HTTPS (the large line files are release assets: see the release page).

Keep the code columns as text when you read a CSV yourself (`hs8`, `hs6`, `chapter`, `month`,
`period`, `tin`): `load_tables` does this for you.

Calibration line, printed under every rate: random draw finds something on 1 to 8 lines in a hundred;
this set is enriched on the 20 exercise lines only.

## Data versions

The tables are generated from one seed, so the same version always holds the same numbers. Each
release (`data-v1`, …, `data-v7`) is fixed. The workshop laptops download its `workshop-data.zip`
(CSV, worksheets, workbook and dictionary) when the workshop kit is set up.
